Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT

Theorem A informally states that Jordan measurable sets are precisely the sets for which you can measure their volume. In other words, you can actually integrate their indicator function. This theorem is a special case of the ultimate theorem on integration which you shall soon learn. To prepare, you can continue to analyze the big ideas of its proof in the harder direction.
\[Theorem A1. If \(S \in \mathbb{R}^n\) is Jordan measurable, then its indicator function \chi_S is integrable on any rectangle \(R\) containing \(S\).\]

Fill in the blanks for this informal summary of the proof.

The upper and lower sums of \(\chi_S\) only differ near \{ $popup1->menu() \}

END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;

install_problem_grader(~~&std_problem_grader);

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ANS( $popup1->cmp() );

